          heading: "Obstructive shock — the block that fluid cannot fix",
          paragraphs: [
            "<strong>Tension pneumothorax</strong> develops when air enters the pleural space through a one-way valve — a stab or gunshot, a fractured rib tearing the lung, a ruptured bulla in an asthmatic or a patient with COPD, a central line attempt, or positive-pressure ventilation of an injured lung — and cannot escape. Each breath adds air, the lung collapses, the mediastinum is pushed across, the great veins kink and venous return falls. The patient is <strong>severely breathless and agitated, with absent breath sounds and a hyper-resonant percussion note on one side, distended neck veins, a trachea deviated away from the affected side (a late sign), a fast pulse and a falling blood pressure</strong>; in a ventilated patient the airway pressures climb and the saturation and pressure fall. It is a <strong>clinical diagnosis treated before any X-ray</strong>: <strong>needle decompression</strong> with a large cannula (14 G, long enough in a muscular adult) in the <strong>second intercostal space in the mid-clavicular line</strong> just above the third rib, or the fifth intercostal space in the anterior axillary line, releases a hiss of air and the pressure recovers; a <strong>chest drain</strong> with an underwater seal follows immediately, because the needle is a temporary measure that may block or displace. The nurse who hears 'absent breath sounds on the right, neck veins up, pressure falling' in a trauma patient should already be opening the cannula.",
            "<strong>Cardiac tamponade</strong> is blood or fluid in the pericardial sac compressing the heart so that it cannot fill — after a stab wound to the chest or upper abdomen (the classic Ghanaian presentation after a fight or a robbery), a myocardial rupture after infarction, tuberculous or malignant pericardial effusion, uraemia, or a central line perforation. <strong>Beck's triad</strong> is hypotension, distended neck veins and muffled heart sounds; add a fast pulse, <strong>pulsus paradoxus</strong> (the radial pulse fades or the systolic falls by more than 10 mmHg on inspiration), a narrow pulse pressure, breathlessness, restlessness, and a patient who leans forward. The X-ray may be normal in acute tamponade because 150 mL is enough to kill; bedside ultrasound shows the fluid and the collapsing right atrium. The treatment is <strong>pericardiocentesis</strong> — a needle under the xiphisternum directed towards the left shoulder, ideally ultrasound-guided, removing even 20–50 mL to restore output — followed by thoracotomy for the stabbed heart; <strong>fluid is given as a bridge</strong> to raise the filling pressure (unlike cardiogenic shock, where it harms), and the patient is never laid flat if they cannot tolerate it or sedated heavily before drainage. In a penetrating chest injury with shock and distended neck veins the question is only 'tension or tamponade?' — breath sounds answer it.",
            "<strong>Massive pulmonary embolism</strong> blocks the pulmonary arteries with a clot from the deep veins of the leg or pelvis — in the post-operative patient on day five, the woman two weeks after a caesarean, the patient with cancer, the long-distance traveller, the patient with sickle cell disease, the immobile stroke patient — and produces <strong>sudden collapse with breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain, haemoptysis, a fast pulse, hypoxia that improves little with oxygen, distended neck veins, a clear chest and a swollen tender calf</strong>. Hypotension or arrest means a massive embolism, and the treatment is <strong>thrombolysis</strong> (streptokinase or alteplase) where it is available and not contraindicated, anticoagulation with heparin for the rest, oxygen, a cautious fluid challenge (500 mL — more over-distends the failing right ventricle), and vasopressors; the nurse's role is recognition, since the diagnosis is missed when the collapse is blamed on 'a heart attack' or 'anxiety', and prevention, since every immobile patient on every ward needs early mobilisation, hydration, stockings and prophylactic heparin where indicated. Pulmonary embolism is one of the <strong>4 Ts of reversible cardiac arrest</strong>, and so are tension pneumothorax and tamponade — the three obstructive shocks are the three 'T's that can be fixed, if someone thinks of them.",
          ],
